Subject: [PATCH] feat: add tofu/ -- a reusable OpenTofu module to provision
 kube-shim on UpCloud

Closes #72. A real, verified-as-far-as-possible-without-a-live-apply
OpenTofu module: upcloud_server (the host) + upcloud_firewall_rules
(22/443/80 inbound, explicit deny-all else) + tls_private_key (the
shim's own worker-VM SSH keypair) + random_password (bearer tokens),
with first-boot provisioning fully automated via user_data/cloud-init
-- every step lifted directly from bootstrap/provision.sh's own
already-hands-on-verified sequence, plus the one step that script
leaves to a human (starting kube-shim.service via machinectl shell),
now automated and retried against a first-boot timing race.

Two real bugs caught by actually rendering the templates against a
scratch root config and checking the output, not by inspection alone:
cloud-init.sh.tftpl's own bash ${VAR} references collided with
templatefile()'s identical interpolation syntax (caught by `tofu
validate`); tls_private_key's public_key_openssh output carries a
trailing newline that landed inside a single-line TOML basic string,
invalid TOML that would have failed to parse at kube-shim startup
(caught by feeding the rendered config.toml through this project's
own real Config::from_file parser).

Deliberately out of scope, documented rather than assumed: DNS record
creation (provider-specific, left to the calling root config), minting
a scoped UpCloud sub-account token (no resource for this exists in
UpCloud's own OpenTofu provider), and whether a user_data-only apply
needs an explicit reboot to take effect (UpCloud's provider docs don't
say, not checked against a real apply).

Never run against a real UpCloud account/token -- see tofu/README.md's
own "Verified vs. not" section.

