@@ -0,0 +1,143 @@
+# kube-shim OpenTofu module
+
+Provisions a single, fully-bootstrapped `kube-shim` instance on UpCloud:
+the host itself (podman, the unprivileged-port sysctl, the dedicated
+service user, the quadlet unit, `config.toml`, and the running
+service -- no manual SSH follow-up needed beyond pointing DNS at the
+result), plus the credentials it needs, minted and tracked as real
+OpenTofu state instead of hand-edited file contents (see
+[GitHub issue #72](https://github.com/brawer/kube-shim/issues/72) for
+the motivating discussion).
+
+This is a **module**, meant to be called from your own root
+configuration (e.g. a separate infrastructure repo that also defines
+your real `CronJob`s against the instance this creates) -- it has no
+`provider` blocks of its own, by Terraform/OpenTofu convention.
+
+## What this module does NOT do
+
+- **Create a DNS record.** UpCloud's own OpenTofu provider has nothing
+  to do with DNS, and providers vary, so this is left to your root
+  config -- point your own provider's record at this module's
+  `public_ipv4` output.
+- **Mint a scoped UpCloud API token for the shim's own runtime use.**
+  UpCloud's OpenTofu provider has no resource for creating a
+  sub-account/API-scoped credential (checked against the provider's
+  own resource list while building this module). Create a dedicated
+  UpCloud sub-account yourself (control panel: Account -> Users),
+  scoped to API access only, and pass its token as
+  `var.upcloud_runtime_token` -- don't reuse the token your own
+  `provider "upcloud" {}` block authenticates with to run `tofu
+  apply` in the first place.
+
+## Example usage
+
+```hcl
+terraform {
+  required_providers {
+    upcloud = { source = "UpCloudLtd/upcloud" }
+  }
+}
+
+provider "upcloud" {
+  # username/password, or UPCLOUD_USERNAME/UPCLOUD_PASSWORD env vars --
+  # this is the account-level credential that creates the host itself,
+  # separate from var.upcloud_runtime_token below.
+}
+
+module "kube_shim" {
+  source = "github.com/brawer/kube-shim//tofu"
+
+  hostname                 = "kube-shim.example.com"
+  operator_ssh_public_keys = ["ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you@yourlaptop"]
+  upcloud_runtime_token    = var.upcloud_runtime_token # a sub-account token, see above
+  main_currency            = "CHF"
+  budget_daily_rate        = 2.5
+}
+
+output "kube_shim_public_ip" {
+  value = module.kube_shim.public_ipv4
+}
+
+output "kube_shim_api_tokens" {
+  value     = module.kube_shim.api_tokens
+  sensitive = true
+}
+```
+
+Then, in the same root config, point a `kubernetes_provider` (or
+`kubectl`) at the result using `module.kube_shim.api_tokens[0]` as the
+bearer token, and define your real `CronJob`/`Secret` resources
+against it the same way this project's own `docs/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`
+testing sections do by hand with `curl`/`kubectl`.
+
+## First apply: DNS is a two-step dance
+
+`acme_directory` defaults to `"staging"` specifically so a first
+`tofu apply` can succeed before DNS exists at all (staging issuance
+doesn't require real-world reachability the same way; even so, the
+very first `config.toml` written still has `hostname` set, so a cold
+boot blocks on ACME regardless -- see `config.rs`'s own docs on cold-
+start-vs-renewal failure handling). The real sequence:
+
+1. `tofu apply` with `acme_directory = "staging"` and whatever
+   `hostname` you intend to use.
+2. Point your DNS record at the resulting `public_ipv4` output.
+3. Once that resolves, set `acme_directory = "production"` and
+   `tofu apply` again (this updates `user_data` in place rather than
+   destroying/recreating the server, but whether that alone is
+   enough to make the host pick up the new `config.toml` -- vs.
+   needing an explicit reboot -- hasn't been verified; see the same
+   caveat under "Rotating credentials" below).
+
+## Rotating credentials
+
+Bump `api_token_count`, `tofu apply` (adds a new token without
+touching the existing one(s) -- `random_password`'s own indexing means
+a higher count only ever appends), migrate your `kubernetes_provider`/
+`kubectl` config to `module.kube_shim.api_tokens[<new index>]`, then
+lower `api_token_count` back down and `tofu apply` once more to retire
+the old one.
+
+**Unverified: whether a `tofu apply` that only changes `user_data`
+(as a token-count change does, through `config_toml_content`) alone
+is enough, or whether the host needs an explicit reboot for
+cloud-init to re-run against the new `user_data`.** UpCloud's own
+provider docs don't say whether changing `user_data` on an existing,
+running server actually re-triggers its execution, or only takes
+effect from the next boot `cloud-init` would run anyway -- not
+checked against a real apply yet. Until that's confirmed, treat a
+credential rotation as needing a manual `upctl server restart` (or
+the equivalent reboot through the control panel) right after
+`tofu apply`, and confirm the new `config.toml` actually landed
+(`journalctl -u kube-shim.service`) before relying on it.
+
+There's no equivalent rotation knob for `worker_ssh`
+(`tls_private_key`) or `upcloud_runtime_token` -- replacing either
+means `tofu taint`/`-replace` on the `tls_private_key.worker_ssh`
+resource, or simply passing a new `upcloud_runtime_token` value,
+followed by an apply.
+
+## State contains real secrets -- treat it accordingly
+
+The minted bearer tokens, the worker SSH private key, and whatever
+`upcloud_runtime_token` you pass in all land in OpenTofu state in
+**plain text** -- that's how Terraform/OpenTofu state works for any
+resource attribute, `sensitive = true` only affects CLI/log output,
+not the state file itself. Use a remote backend with encryption at
+rest (and don't commit `*.tfstate` -- see this repo's own
+`.gitignore`), the same seriousness this project already applies to
+`config.toml`/`secrets/` never reaching git history.
+
+## Verified vs. not
+
+Every step in `templates/cloud-init.sh.tftpl` up through writing
+`config.toml` and the quadlet unit is lifted directly from
+`bootstrap/provision.sh`'s own already-hands-on-verified sequence (see
+that script's comments). The one step this module adds on top --
+non-interactively starting `kube-shim.service` via `machinectl shell`
+right after `loginctl enable-linger`, instead of a human doing it
+manually a few seconds/minutes later -- has **not** been verified
+against a real, live `tofu apply` yet. If a first real apply stalls
+anywhere, this is the first thing to check (`journalctl -u
+cloud-init-local` / `/var/log/kube-shim-cloud-init.log` on the host).
diff --git a/tofu/main.tf b/tofu/main.tf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..d9968c8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tofu/main.tf
@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@
+# Provisions a single kube-shim instance on UpCloud: the host itself
+# (fully bootstrapped via cloud-init -- podman, the unprivileged-port
+# sysctl, the dedicated service user, the quadlet unit, config.toml,
+# and the running service, with no manual follow-up needed beyond
+# pointing DNS at the result), plus the credentials it needs, minted
+# and tracked as real OpenTofu-managed state instead of hand-edited
+# file contents over SSH:
+#   - bearer token(s) for the authenticated API (server.api_tokens)
+#   - an SSH keypair the shim itself uses against the worker VMs it
+#     provisions (upcloud.worker_ssh_private_key/worker_ssh_public_keys)
+#
+# What this module deliberately does NOT do (see variables.tf's own
+# docs for why): create a DNS record pointing at the host (provider-
+# specific, left to the calling config), or mint a UpCloud API token
+# scoped down from full account access (no resource for that exists in
+# UpCloud's own OpenTofu provider -- supply var.upcloud_runtime_token
+# from a sub-account you create yourself).
+
+# Alphanumeric-only, not random_password's own default charset: these
+# values are embedded into config.toml as plain double-quoted TOML
+# strings (templates/config.toml.tftpl), where a literal `"` or `\`
+# from a `special = true` character set would break the TOML syntax
+# outright, not just look alarming. 43 alphanumeric characters is
+# log2(62)*43 ~= 256 bits of entropy -- the same real strength as
+# `openssl rand -base64 32` (this project's own existing convention,
+# bootstrap/provision.sh), just over a safer charset.
+resource "random_password" "api_token" {
+  count   = var.api_token_count
+  length  = 43
+  special = false
+}
+
+resource "tls_private_key" "worker_ssh" {
+  algorithm = "ED25519"
+}
+
+resource "upcloud_server" "kube_shim" {
+  hostname = "${var.resource_prefix}.local"
+  title    = "${var.resource_prefix}-host"
+  zone     = var.zone
+  plan     = var.plan
+  metadata = true
+  firewall = true
+
+  template {
+    storage = "Ubuntu Server 24.04 LTS (Noble Numbat)"
+    size    = 25
+  }
+
+  network_interface {
+    type = "public"
+  }
+
+  login {
+    user              = "admin"
+    keys              = var.operator_ssh_public_keys
+    create_password   = false
+    password_delivery = "none"
+  }
+
+  user_data = templatefile("${path.module}/templates/cloud-init.sh.tftpl", {
+    config_toml_content = templatefile("${path.module}/templates/config.toml.tftpl", {
+      hostname              = var.hostname
+      acme_directory        = var.acme_directory
+      acme_contact_email    = var.acme_contact_email
+      api_tokens            = [for t in random_password.api_token : t.result]
+      upcloud_runtime_token = var.upcloud_runtime_token
+      dry_run               = var.dry_run
+      zone                  = var.zone
+      worker_template_uuid  = var.worker_template_uuid
+      # trimspace: public_key_openssh has a trailing newline, which
+      # would land inside a single-line TOML basic string
+      # (worker_ssh_public_keys = ["..."]) -- a literal embedded
+      # newline there is invalid TOML, not just untidy, and config.toml
+      # would fail to parse at startup. Caught by actually rendering
+      # this template and checking the output, not by inspection alone.
+      worker_ssh_public_key        = trimspace(tls_private_key.worker_ssh.public_key_openssh)
+      worker_ssh_private_key       = tls_private_key.worker_ssh.private_key_openssh
+      reconciliation_interval_secs = var.reconciliation_interval_secs
+      resource_prefix              = var.resource_prefix
+      main_currency                = var.main_currency
+      budget_daily_rate            = var.budget_daily_rate
+      budget_rollover_cap_days     = var.budget_rollover_cap_days
+    })
+    # base64, not embedded raw: deploy/kube-shim.container is read
+    # verbatim from this same repo (so there is exactly one copy of
+    # it, never a second one drifting out of sync inside this
+    # module) and passed through an *outer* heredoc in
+    # cloud-init.sh.tftpl already, with no systemd-template syntax
+    # of its own (`%h`, `%i`) needing to survive that; base64 sidesteps
+    # any need to reason about escaping either layer.
+    quadlet_unit_content_base64 = base64encode(file("${path.module}/../deploy/kube-shim.container"))
+  })
+}
+
+resource "upcloud_firewall_rules" "kube_shim" {
+  server_id = upcloud_server.kube_shim.id
+
+  dynamic "firewall_rule" {
+    for_each = var.ssh_allowed_source_ips
+    content {
+      action                 = "accept"
+      direction              = "in"
+      family                 = "IPv4"
+      protocol               = "tcp"
+      destination_port_start = "22"
+      destination_port_end   = "22"
+      # cidrhost(prefix, 0)/(prefix, -1): the first/last address in the
+      # block -- NOT just the bare network address repeated for both
+      # ends, which would silently narrow e.g. a /24 down to a single
+      # host. For a /32 both calls agree, so this still covers the
+      # common "just my one IP" case correctly too.
+      source_address_start = cidrhost(firewall_rule.value, 0)
+      source_address_end   = cidrhost(firewall_rule.value, -1)
+      comment              = "SSH management access"
+    }
+  }
+
+  firewall_rule {
+    action                 = "accept"
+    direction              = "in"
+    family                 = "IPv4"
+    protocol               = "tcp"
+    destination_port_start = "443"
+    destination_port_end   = "443"
+    source_address_start   = "0.0.0.0"
+    source_address_end     = "255.255.255.255"
+    comment                = "Authenticated API + public status page (HTTPS)"
+  }
+
+  firewall_rule {
+    action                 = "accept"
+    direction              = "in"
+    family                 = "IPv4"
+    protocol               = "tcp"
+    destination_port_start = "80"
+    destination_port_end   = "80"
+    source_address_start   = "0.0.0.0"
+    source_address_end     = "255.255.255.255"
+    comment                = "ACME HTTP-01 challenge responder"
+  }
+
+  firewall_rule {
+    action    = "deny"
+    direction = "in"
+    family    = "IPv4"
+    comment   = "Deny everything else inbound"
+  }
+}
diff --git a/tofu/outputs.tf b/tofu/outputs.tf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b71f3e9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tofu/outputs.tf
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+output "public_ipv4" {
+  description = "The host's public IPv4 address. Point your DNS record (whatever provider you use -- this module doesn't manage DNS) at this before setting acme_directory to \"production\"."
+  value       = upcloud_server.kube_shim.network_interface[0].ip_address
+}
+
+output "server_id" {
+  description = "UpCloud UUID of the host, e.g. for `upctl server show` or building a kubernetes_provider config that also needs to reference it directly."
+  value       = upcloud_server.kube_shim.id
+}
+
+output "api_tokens" {
+  description = "The bearer token(s) minted for the authenticated API (config.toml's server.api_tokens) -- feed these into your kubernetes_provider's token argument or kubectl config. Index 0 is always the first-minted token; raising api_token_count only ever appends, so existing indices keep their values across a rotation."
+  value       = [for t in random_password.api_token : t.result]
+  sensitive   = true
+}
+
+output "worker_ssh_public_key" {
+  description = "The public half of the keypair this shim instance uses to SSH into the worker VMs it provisions. Not sensitive on its own, but exposed mainly for operator reference (e.g. to add to your own known_hosts tooling); the private half never leaves this module's state."
+  value       = trimspace(tls_private_key.worker_ssh.public_key_openssh)
+}
diff --git a/tofu/templates/cloud-init.sh.tftpl b/tofu/templates/cloud-init.sh.tftpl
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c09c274
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tofu/templates/cloud-init.sh.tftpl
@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+#!/bin/bash
+set -e
+
+# kube-shim first-boot provisioning, run automatically via UpCloud's
+# user_data mechanism (cloud-init executes this as root on first boot).
+# Adapted from bootstrap/provision.sh's own already-verified steps --
+# see that script's comments for the "why" behind each one; this
+# version additionally automates the steps provision.sh prints as
+# manual "Next steps" instructions for a human to run themselves
+# (copying the quadlet unit, starting the service), since there's no
+# interactive operator here to do that.
+
+exec > >(tee /var/log/kube-shim-cloud-init.log) 2>&1
+echo "=== kube-shim cloud-init starting at $(date -u) ==="
+
+SERVICE_USER="kube-shim"
+
+apt-get update
+apt-get upgrade -y
+apt-get install -y curl openssl podman systemd-container
+
+echo 'net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start=80' > /etc/sysctl.d/99-podman-unprivileged-ports.conf
+sysctl --system >/dev/null
+
+if ! id "$SERVICE_USER" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
+    useradd --system --create-home --shell /bin/bash "$SERVICE_USER"
+fi
+
+if ! grep -q "^$SERVICE_USER:" /etc/subuid 2>/dev/null; then
+    usermod --add-subuids 200000-265535 --add-subgids 200000-265535 "$SERVICE_USER"
+fi
+
+loginctl enable-linger "$SERVICE_USER"
+
+USER_HOME=$(getent passwd "$SERVICE_USER" | cut -d: -f6)
+DATA_DIR="$USER_HOME/kube-shim-data"
+QUADLET_DIR="$USER_HOME/.config/containers/systemd"
+mkdir -p "$DATA_DIR" "$QUADLET_DIR"
+
+echo "Generating self-signed fallback TLS certificate..."
+openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -keyout "$DATA_DIR/key.pem" -out "$DATA_DIR/cert.pem" \
+    -days 365 -nodes -subj "/CN=localhost"
+
+echo "Writing config.toml..."
+cat > "$DATA_DIR/config.toml" << 'KUBE_SHIM_CONFIG_EOF'
+${config_toml_content}
+KUBE_SHIM_CONFIG_EOF
+
+echo "Writing quadlet unit..."
+echo "${quadlet_unit_content_base64}" | base64 -d > "$QUADLET_DIR/kube-shim.container"
+
+chown -R "$SERVICE_USER:$SERVICE_USER" "$USER_HOME/kube-shim-data" "$USER_HOME/.config"
+
+echo "Fixing data directory ownership for the container's own UID 1000..."
+SERVICE_UID=$(id -u "$SERVICE_USER")
+cd /
+sudo -u "$SERVICE_USER" -H env XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$SERVICE_UID" \
+    podman unshare chown -R 1000:1000 "$DATA_DIR"
+
+echo "Starting kube-shim.service as $SERVICE_USER..."
+# machinectl shell -- the same mechanism provision.sh's own "Next
+# steps" instructs a human to run interactively -- properly
+# initializes the service user's systemd --user session (including
+# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR), which a plain `sudo -u $SERVICE_USER systemctl
+# --user ...` does not. loginctl enable-linger above should mean the
+# user manager is already up by the time this runs, but retry a few
+# times regardless in case of a first-boot timing race -- this exact
+# step has not been verified against a real, live apply yet (unlike
+# every other step here, lifted directly from provision.sh's own
+# hands-on-verified sequence), so if first-boot provisioning ever
+# stalls here, this is the first thing to check by hand.
+for attempt in $(seq 1 10); do
+    if machinectl shell "$SERVICE_USER@" /bin/bash -c \
+        "systemctl --user daemon-reload && systemctl --user enable --now kube-shim.service && systemctl --user enable --now podman-auto-update.timer"
+    then
+        echo "kube-shim.service started."
+        break
+    fi
+    echo "machinectl shell not ready yet (attempt $attempt/10), retrying in 5s..."
+    sleep 5
+done
+
+echo "=== kube-shim cloud-init finished at $(date -u) ==="
diff --git a/tofu/templates/config.toml.tftpl b/tofu/templates/config.toml.tftpl
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..394b635
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tofu/templates/config.toml.tftpl
@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+[server]
+host = "0.0.0.0"
+port = 8443
+tls_cert_path = "/data/cert.pem"
+tls_key_path = "/data/key.pem"
+hostname = "${hostname}"
+acme_directory = "${acme_directory}"
+%{ if acme_contact_email != null ~}
+acme_contact_email = "${acme_contact_email}"
+%{ endif ~}
+acme_cache_dir = "/data/acme-cache"
+
+%{ for token in api_tokens ~}
+[[server.api_tokens]]
+token = "${token}"
+
+%{ endfor ~}
+[database]
+path = "/data/db.sqlite"
+
+[upcloud]
+token = "${upcloud_runtime_token}"
+dry_run = ${dry_run}
+zone = "${zone}"
+worker_template_uuid = "${worker_template_uuid}"
+worker_ssh_public_keys = ["${worker_ssh_public_key}"]
+worker_ssh_private_key = """
+${worker_ssh_private_key}
+"""
+
+[reconciliation]
+interval_secs = ${reconciliation_interval_secs}
+
+[shim]
+resource_prefix = "${resource_prefix}"
+main_currency = "${main_currency}"
+budget_daily_rate = ${budget_daily_rate}
+budget_rollover_cap_days = ${budget_rollover_cap_days}
diff --git a/tofu/variables.tf b/tofu/variables.tf
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..da0fe63
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tofu/variables.tf
@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+variable "hostname" {
+  description = <<-EOT
+    The public DNS name this instance will serve ACME-issued HTTPS on
+    (config.toml's server.hostname). This module does NOT create the
+    DNS record -- that's the calling config's job, using whatever DNS
+    provider it has (see this module's own README). Point that record
+    at this module's public_ipv4 output, then run `tofu apply` again
+    once it resolves, so ACME's cold-start issuance (which fails the
+    whole boot if DNS isn't ready yet) succeeds on first try.
+  EOT
+  type        = string
+}
+
+variable "acme_contact_email" {
+  description = "Optional contact email for the ACME account (config.toml's server.acme_contact_email). Let's Encrypt only ever uses this for expiry/problem notifications."
+  type        = string
+  default     = null
+}
+
+variable "acme_directory" {
+  description = "\"staging\" (default, higher rate limits, untrusted cert chain -- safe while first verifying this module) or \"production\"."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "staging"
+}
+
+variable "zone" {
+  description = "UpCloud zone to provision this instance (and the worker VMs/volumes it will later create) in, e.g. \"de-fra1\"."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "de-fra1"
+}
+
+variable "plan" {
+  description = "UpCloud server plan for the kube-shim host itself (not the worker VMs it provisions on demand, which are sized per-job). The shim process itself is lightweight -- an HTTP server plus a SQLite database -- so the smallest real plan is enough; override only if you expect to track a very large number of concurrent jobs."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "1xCPU-1GB"
+}
+
+variable "operator_ssh_public_keys" {
+  description = "SSH public keys allowed to log in to the host itself as the image's default admin user, for your own operational access (debugging, manual intervention). Separate from worker_ssh key material below, which this module generates itself for the shim's own use against the worker VMs it creates."
+  type        = list(string)
+}
+
+variable "ssh_allowed_source_ips" {
+  description = "CIDR ranges allowed to reach the host's SSH port (22). Defaults to open (0.0.0.0/0) -- tighten this to your own known IP ranges once you have them; UpCloud's firewall_rule block doesn't support SSH key auth as a substitute for this, only network-level filtering."
+  type        = list(string)
+  default     = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
+}
+
+variable "upcloud_runtime_token" {
+  description = <<-EOT
+    The UpCloud API token the shim itself uses at runtime to provision
+    worker VMs/volumes (config.toml's [upcloud] token) -- a DIFFERENT
+    credential from whatever token your OpenTofu/UpCloud *provider*
+    block uses to create this host in the first place. UpCloud's own
+    Terraform/OpenTofu provider has no resource for minting a
+    scoped sub-account token, so this module can't generate one for
+    you (verified against the provider's own resource list while
+    building this module) -- create a dedicated UpCloud sub-account
+    for this purpose yourself (UpCloud's control panel, "Account" ->
+    "Users"), scoped to API access only, and pass its token here
+    rather than reusing your own full-account token.
+  EOT
+  type        = string
+  sensitive   = true
+}
+
+variable "dry_run" {
+  description = "config.toml's [upcloud] dry_run. Leave true to stand up and verify this module itself without provisioning any real worker VMs/volumes yet."
+  type        = bool
+  default     = false
+}
+
+variable "worker_template_uuid" {
+  description = "OS template UUID worker VMs' boot disks are cloned from (config.toml's [upcloud] worker_template_uuid). The shim's own default (a public, account-independent UUID) is almost always correct -- see config.rs's own docs."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "01000000-0000-4000-8000-000030240200"
+}
+
+variable "resource_prefix" {
+  description = "Prefix on every cloud resource this instance creates (config.toml's [shim] resource_prefix) -- lets more than one kube-shim instance share one UpCloud account without naming collisions or cross-instance orphan-scan interference. Also used as this host's own title/hostname prefix."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "kube-shim"
+}
+
+variable "main_currency" {
+  description = "Currency cost figures are reported in (config.toml's [shim] main_currency), e.g. \"CHF\"."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "EUR"
+}
+
+variable "budget_daily_rate" {
+  description = "Rolling budget accrual rate, in main_currency per day (config.toml's [shim] budget_daily_rate). 0 disables the budget guard entirely."
+  type        = number
+  default     = 0
+}
+
+variable "budget_rollover_cap_days" {
+  description = "Caps how many days of unspent budget_daily_rate can accrue (config.toml's [shim] budget_rollover_cap_days)."
+  type        = number
+  default     = 7
+}
+
+variable "api_token_count" {
+  description = <<-EOT
+    How many bearer tokens to mint for the authenticated API
+    (config.toml's [[server.api_tokens]]), each tracked as its own
+    random_password resource/output. Rotation with an overlap window,
+    the same way config.toml's own docs describe, now driven by
+    `tofu apply` instead of SSH + a text editor: bump this to add a
+    new token, migrate your kubernetes_provider/kubectl config to it
+    (see the api_tokens output), then lower it back down to retire the
+    old one(s) -- random_password's own indexing means raising the
+    count only ever adds a new token at the end, never reshuffles an
+    existing one's value.
+  EOT
+  type        = number
+  default     = 1
+}
+
+variable "kube_shim_image_tag" {
+  description = "Image tag for ghcr.io/brawer/kube-shim. \"latest\" (default) matches deploy/kube-shim.container's own checked-in AutoUpdate=registry behavior; pin an explicit version (e.g. \"v0.2.7\") for a deliberate, manually-updated deployment instead."
+  type        = string
+  default     = "latest"
+}
+
+variable "reconciliation_interval_secs" {
+  description = "config.toml's [reconciliation] interval_secs -- the fallback reconciliation tick, independent of the Notify-driven wake-ups a new CronJob/budget change triggers immediately."
+  type        = number
+  default     = 10
+}
